-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I18: an interface reached ONLY through a module ALIAS.
--
-- 🚨 THIS ROW PINS A KNOWN-INCONSISTENT PRE-EXISTING ANSWER. It is NOT a claim that 7 is
-- correct. `import smod as S` cannot put a TYPE name in scope -- an alias-qualified name in
-- type position is a parse error -- and writing `impl Sizeable Blob` in this module is
-- rejected `Unknown interface: Sizeable`. By S1's own criterion the interface is therefore
-- not nameable here, `size` is not a shadow, and the explicitly imported standalone should
-- answer 99. It answers 7, on this binary AND on the base compiler.
--
-- WHY IT IS PINNED ANYWAY, AND WHY IT WAS NOT "FIXED": the discriminating information does
-- not exist by the time any rule here could use it. `renameAliasedMethods` rewrites `S.size`
-- to a BARE `size`, so an alias-qualified call and a bare reference to the imported
-- standalone become the SAME token -- see `both.mdk` beside this file, which contains both
-- spellings and shows `run` cannot tell them apart at all. An attempt to condition the alias
-- rule on "names this module otherwise binds" produced `run` = 99 against a built binary of
-- 7, at exit 0 with no diagnostic: a NEW silent divergence, strictly worse than the
-- inconsistency it tried to remove. Repairing this needs provenance restored to the
-- occurrence upstream (A-3 / #1112), not another rule over import clauses.
--
-- The row self-drains: the day the alias axis is fixed this goes RED and is re-graded to 99.
import fmod.{size}
import smod as S

main = println (size "abc")
